{"title":"The Demo Illusion","markdown":{"headingText":"The Demo Illusion","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-the-demo-illusion","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> \"It works\" is the beginning of the sentence, not the end.\n\n**Lab companion:** [Lab 2, The Demo Illusion](https://michael-borck.github.io/programming-labs/lab-02-demo-illusion/) — this chapter is the reasoning; the lab is the doing.\n\n## The Concept First\n\nThe agent shows you a working page and says it is done. You look, you click, it works. The illusion is complete — and it was built in seconds, which is exactly what makes it dangerous.\n\nHere is the truth the demo hides: **a demo is one path through the software, walked once, by the person who already knows where the stepping stones are.** The agent typed your name correctly; it did not type two hundred characters of emoji. It pressed the button in the right order; it did not press it twice, or press back halfway, or lose the network at the wrong moment.\n\nSoftware is not done when one path works. It is done when the paths you never imagined have been tried — and the only reliable way to try them all is to write them down *before* the software exists.\n\n## Understanding Through the Coffee Shop\n\nA barista makes your usual perfectly. Is the cafe good? One order proves almost nothing. The cafe is good when the strange orders work: the decaf-oat-extra-hot-half-sweet, the order for here that changes to takeaway, the two customers who arrive at once.\n\nSoftware is a cafe full of strange orders. The agent built you a signup form — but nobody has yet:\n\n- Submitted it empty\n- Submitted it twice, fast\n- Typed an email with a doubled `@`\n- Typed an address so long it broke the layout\n- Pressed submit on a slow train, then again when nothing seemed to happen\n\nEvery one of those is a real customer. The demo met none of them.\n\n## Discovering the Illusion with Your AI Partner\n\n### Exploration 1: The Agent Tries to Break It\n\nThe agent built the thing; let the agent attack it. This reversal is the chapter's core move:\n\n```text\nYou built this signup page for me. Now act as a hostile quality\ntester. List every way a real user could break it — strange inputs,\ndouble actions, empty everything. Do not fix anything yet. Just list.\n```\n\n### Exploration 2: Define Done First\n\nNow — before a single fix — write the acceptance list:\n\n```text\nUsing your hostile list, write an acceptance checklist for this page.\nEach item must be a check that can be run and pass or fail — not \"the\npage should be robust\" but \"submitting with an empty name shows an\nerror message and does not send data.\"\n```\n\n::: {.callout-important title=\"Runnable or it is a wish\"}\n\"Should handle bad input\" is a wish. \"Submitting an empty form shows an error and nothing is sent\" is a check. The difference is that a check can be run by someone who was not in the conversation — including a script, including a fresh agent, including you in three months. The whole discipline of this book is turning wishes into checks.\n:::\n\n### Exploration 3: The Red, Green Record\n\nFix nothing until the checklist exists. Then hand the checklist back:\n\n```text\nHere is my acceptance checklist. For each item, run it and report\nPASS or FAIL with what you observed. Then fix the failing items, and\nreport each one passing. Do not mark anything PASS that you did not\nrun.\n```\n\nWhat you have just done is a miniature of professional software delivery — requirements agreed before the build, verification after, nothing accepted on vibes. The agent will sometimes report a pass that is not real. Check one at random. Every time.\n\n## From Concept to Code\n\nThe record of this chapter is not the fixed page. It is the checklist, filed where the department keeps its records:\n\n```bash\n# The checklist is a first-class document, not a chat memory\ngit add ACCEPTANCE.md\ngit commit -m \"Add signup acceptance checklist: 8 runnable checks\"\n```\n\nNow the page, the checks, and the fixes form one story that anyone — a client, a colleague, a future you — can read without trusting anyone's memory of a chat.\n\n## Business Connection\n\nThe demo illusion is where AI-era projects die commercially. A stakeholder sees the demo, assumes the product, and budgets for launch. Then the strange orders arrive — real users, real inputs — and the \"working\" software costs triple to repair, under deadline, with the credibility already spent.\n\nDefining done before building is the cheapest insurance in software: it costs minutes at briefing time and buys the ability to say, with evidence, \"here is what works, here is what does not yet, and here is what it will cost.\"\n\n## Practice Exercises\n\n**Exercise 3.1 (Level 1).** Take the agent's signup page. Run the acceptance checklist yourself. Record one PASS and one FAIL.\n\n**Exercise 3.2 (Level 2).** Add three strange orders of your own to the checklist — orders no one listed.\n\n**Exercise 3.3 (Level 3).** Ask the agent to break the page on purpose, then to explain each break. Fix them with the agent, one at a time.\n\n**Exercise 3.4 (Level 4).** A stakeholder says: \"the demo worked, why isn't it live?\" Write the reply that shows the gap using your checklist — no jargon.\n\n**Exercise 3.5 (Level 5).** Design \"definition of done\" for a real project you care about: ten checks, each runnable, none a wish. File it in the repo.\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n- A demo is one path, walked once, by someone who knew the path\n- Strange orders — real users — are what break software\n- Done is defined before building: runnable checks, not wishes\n- The record of the checks is a first-class document in the repository\n\n## Reflection\n\nBefore moving on, ensure you:\n\n- [ ] Watched the agent attack its own work — and found something\n- [ ] Wrote an acceptance checklist where every item can pass or fail\n- [ ] Fixed from the checklist, not from vibes\n- [ ] Committed the checklist itself, not just the fix\n\n## Your Learning Journal\n\n1. What did the hostile tester find that you genuinely did not expect?\n2. Which of your checklist items was secretly a wish?\n3. Where else in your working life are you accepting demos as products?\n\n## Next Steps\n\nYou can now define done. The next chapter is the skill underneath everything: saying what you mean so precisely that a stranger — agent or human — could reproduce the result without you in the room.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","toc":true,"toc-depth":3,"number-sections":true,"highlight-style":"github","output-file":"the-demo-illusion.html"},"language":{"t